# Everything is LF. The exceptions are listed, and there are two.
#
# Naming extensions one at a time is how this went wrong. The rules started
# as *.sh and *.py -- CRLF breaks a shebang (`env: bash\r`) and a
# `\`-continued line -- and then had to grow *.yml, *.json, *.txt and *.md as
# each one was found the hard way. The last of them was static/index.html,
# noticed only because a fresh worktree made to deploy FROM was about to ship
# a CRLF dashboard to fourteen Linux and macOS boxes.
#
# What made it hard to see: with `text` set, git hashes the converted content,
# so a CRLF working-tree copy of an LF blob is "clean" to git status. Thirty-
# nine tracked files were sitting like that, having been checked out before
# the *.py and *.sh rules existed. One was hosts/linux/grants.sh, which
# deploy-gateway.sh runs on every Linux deploy -- and which already carried a
# `sed -i "s/\r$//"` on the receiving end, which is how somebody found this
# the first time.
#
# The cost of getting it wrong is not only a broken script. deploy-gateway.sh
# ships the working tree, and the pi runner is Linux while this workstation is
# Windows, so an unruled file flip-flops between LF and CRLF depending on
# which machine deployed last. public_domains_seed.json differed by 4717
# bytes and not one character; fleetctl duly reported `gateway-files: 4 of 6
# differ` on every box in the fleet, permanently, and content-comparing tools
# cannot tell that apart from a real change.
#
# `text=auto` leaves detected binaries alone, so this is safe as a default and
# covers whatever file type gets added next.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Batch files are the exception that is not a preference: cmd.exe mis-parses a
# .cmd with LF endings. fleetctl writes the wrappers it generates CRLF for the
# same reason (see Ctx._NL, and the UNCHANGED loop it caused on three Windows
# boxes when the check read them back translated).
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binaries, in case the detector is ever unsure about one of them.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.gz   binary
*.zip  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
